  152                                   ;  Table 1: The structure of an 'activobject' in Tempest 2000.
  153                                   ;
  154                                   ; Overview of How Objects Are Created, Updated and Drawn to the Screen
  155                                   ; -------------------------------------------------------------------- 
  156                                   ; Two routines work in lockstep to generate, update, and draw objects in Tempest
  157                                   ; 2000 while a game is in progress. These are:
  158                                   ; 
  159                                   ;  - 'Frame': This routine is a 'vertical sync interrupt handler'. This means
  160                                   ;     that the Atari Jaguar's CPU runs it every time the Object Processor has
  161                                   ;     finished painting the screen and is about to start painting it again. This
  162                                   ;     creates a brief interlude in which game state can be updated and even some
  163                                   ;     preparation performed of the next batch of pixels to be drawn to the screen. Very little
  164                                   ;     pixel preparation is done in 'Frame': it focuses principally on updating the
  165                                   ;     state of all the objects in the game. This routine is called up to 60 times a
  166                                   ;     second.
  167                                   ;
  168                                   ;  - 'mainloop': This routine is the game's main loop. It is responsible for


                                        src/yak.s            Page 4       5:01:00 pm 29-Sep-2026        RMAC 2.1.01 (Linux/OSX)


  169                                   ;     nearly all of the 'drawing' in Tempest 2000. By 'drawing' we mean preparing the
  170                                   ;     pixel data in RAM that the Jaguar's Object Processor will use to paint the
  171                                   ;     screen after the next vertical sync. It relies on 'Frame' to make the state of
  172                                   ;     all objects in the activeobjects list up-to-date so it can iterate through them
  173                                   ;     all and convert that state into pixels.
  174                                   ;
  175                                   ; The mechanism these two routines use to hand off work to each other is the
  176                                   ; 'sync' variable. When 'Frame' has completed updating all the objects, it will
  177                                   ; reset 'sync' to 0. When 'mainloop' sees that 'sync' is 0 it will process all
  178                                   ; the activeobjects and 'draw' them. When it is finished it will set sync to 1,
  179                                   ; so that 'Frame' knows it is time to advance the state of all the objects again.
  180                                   ; 
  181                                   ; 'Frame': Creating Objects 
  182                                   ; -------------------------
  183                                   ; Most objects are created from an unlikely place: the 'moveclaw' routine. For
  184                                   ; most visits to 'Frame' this 'moveclaw' routine will be called (it is possible
  185                                   ; that the game enters other states where moveclaw isn't run, such as high score
  186                                   ; tables etc.). As the name suggests it will move the player's claw, but it will
  187                                   ; also call a routine 'run_wave', and it is in here that most or all of the enemy
  188                                   ; objects are first generated.  Every object the game creates is added to the
  189                                   ; 'activeobjects' list - a master array containing nearly all objects in the game
  190                                   ; (see 'insertobject').
  191                                   ; 
  192                                   ; 'Frame': Updating Objects
  193                                   ;  ------------------------- 
  194                                   ; Once we have an 'activeobjects' list with stuff in it, 'Frame' has a list to
  195                                   ; process every time it is called by the CPU (in addition to generating new
  196                                   ; objects). The routine that does this is called 'run_objects' and it iterates
  197                                   ; through every object in the activeobjects list and runs the given routine for
  198                                   ; that object, e.g.  'run_flipper' for a Flipper object.  Every object points
  199                                   ; to the routine responsible for updating its state in Bytes 54-56. It does
  200                                   ; this using an index into the 'run_vex' array. So for example, a flipper
  201                                   ; object will have a value of 2 in Bytes 54-56 as this points to the third
  202                                   ; element in run_vex: 'run_flipper', a Tanker object will have a value of 5 as
  203                                   ; this points to the sixth element in run_vex: 'run_tanker', and so on.
  204                                   ; 
  205                                   ; Once all the objects in activeobjects have had their 'run_vex' routine
  206                                   ; executed, 'Frame' can reset 'sync' to 0, indicating to 'mainloop' that it's
  207                                   ; time to draw stuff.
  208                                   ; 
  209                                   ; 'mainloop': Drawing Objects 
  210                                   ; --------------------------- 
  211                                   ; While all of this is happening mainloop is running in a loop waiting for 'sync'
  212                                   ; to be reset to 0.  Once it sees that has happened it can finally start work.
  213                                   ; It's main task it run 'draw_objects', which iterates through every object in
  214                                   ; the activeobjects list and call its draw_vex routine. Like 'run_vex' this is
  215                                   ; an index in Bytes 34-36 of the object that it uses to look up the object's draw
  216                                   ; routine in the 'draw_vex' array.
  217                                   ; 
  218                                   ; Each object's routine in draw_vex will do one of the following: 
  219                                   ;  -  draw the object itself (e.g. ). 
  220                                   ;  -  if we are in Tempest Classic mode, draw the simple vector object pointed to by the
  221                                   ;     address pointer in Bytes 0-4.  
  222                                   ;  -  if the value in Bytes 0-4 is negative, treat it as index referencing a solid polygon
  223                                   ;     in the 'solids' list.  In this case drawing is  deferred and the object is added to the 'apriority' list.
  224                                   ; 


                                        src/yak.s            Page 5       5:01:00 pm 29-Sep-2026        RMAC 2.1.01 (Linux/OSX)


  225                                   ; Once 'draw_objects' has processed all the objects in activeobjects, it will
  226                                   ; have drawn some, but not all. It may have deferred drawing on a few and instead
  227                                   ; added them to the 'apriority' list. These objects are always simple solid
  228                                   ; polygons that were constructed when the game was initialized. To draw these it
  229                                   ; will now call 'drawpolyos' which will iterate through every object in the
  230                                   ; 'apriority' list. The routine to draw the polygon for each object is selected
  231                                   ; by treating the value in Bytes 0-4 as an index into the 'solids' list, which
  232                                   ; contains a list of all possible routines for drawing the game's polygon based objects. 
  233                                   ; 
  234                                   ; So to restate the above slightly differently. Drawing has three orders of
  235                                   ; complexity:
  236                                   ;
  237                                   ;  - The object requires customized drawing, so has its own dedicated
  238                                   ;    routine, such as draw_pixex for explosions.
  239                                   ;  - The object is a simple vector-based object, such as a claw or enemy in 
  240                                   ;    Tempest Classic mode, e.g '_flipper'.  
  241                                   ;  - The object is a simple polygon-based object, such as a claw or enemy in 
  242                                   ;    Tempest 2000 mode, e.g. 'cdraw_sflipper'.  
  243                                   ;
  244                                   ; Which of these we use is determined by the routine in 'draw_vex' that
  245                                   ; the object holds an index to in Bytes 34-36. The most commonly used routine
  246                                   ; is 'draw': this selects between option 2 and 3 above. If Bytes 0-4 contain
  247                                   ; an address to a vector object it draws that (in 'vector'), otherwise it
  248                                   ; adds the object to the 'apriority' list so that its solid polygon object
  249                                   ; can by drawn by the routine Bytes 0-4 point to in the 'solids' list. 
  250                                   ; 
  251                                   ; In the hope that it will aid understanding, the table below sets out the
  252                                   ; routines used for creating, updating, and drawing all player and enemy objects
  253                                   ; in the game. For example, you can hopefully see that 'make_tanker' will create
  254                                   ; a Tanker object, and 'Frame' will use 'run_tanker' as the routine to update its
  255                                   ; state, while 'draw_objects' will use 'draw' to draw it to the screen, which will in turn
  256                                   ; use either 'draw_sfliptank' to draw it as a solid polygon in Tempest 2000 mode,
  257                                   ; or the '_fliptank' vector object if in Tempest Classic mode.
  258                                   ;
  259                                   ;  --------------------- -------------- ------------ ----------- -------------  ------------    
  260                                   ;     Description        Creating       Updating     Drawing     Solids          Vectors
  261                                   ;                                       Bytes 54-56  Bytes 34-36 Bytes 0-4       Bytes 0-4
  262                                   ;                                       (run_vex)    draw_vex    solids         (Address)          
  263                                   ;  --------------------- -------------- ------------ ----------- -------------  ------------    
  264                                   ;   0 Exploding Player                  rrts         changex
