  314                                   ;      Table 2: The creation, update, and draw routines used by game elements in Tempest 2000.
  315                                   ;
  316                                   ; If you look at the above table long enough you may realize that not all
  317                                   ; objects are created 'de novo'. Some objects 'turn into' other objects rather
  318                                   ; than get created from scratch by 'run_wave'. For example, an enemy will turn
  319                                   ; into an explosion.
  320                                   ; 
  321                                   ; The management of claw objects is slightly more complex than the table above
  322                                   ; admits. 
  323                                   ;       Single Player Claw      setclaw                 srezclaw->claw_con1       dsclaw            sclaws                 
  324                                   ;
  325                                   ;
  326                                   ; Drawing Things to the Screen
  327                                   ; ----------------------------
  328                                   ; When we describe 'drawing' to a screen above, what we actually mean is
  329                                   ; filling a region in RAM with data that can be used by the Jaguar's Object
  330                                   ; Processor to fill a physical screen that is 384 pixels wide and 280 pixels
  331                                   ; high with the objects that we have drawn. Since each pixel is represented by
  332                                   ; 2 bytes of RAM, this means the region of RAM containing the data necessary to
  333                                   ; draw a full screen is 215040 bytes long (384*280*2). With the width and
  334                                   ; height of the screen fixed in this way, we know that the first 768 bytes in
  335                                   ; this region contain the pixel data for the first line of the screen, the next
  336                                   ; 768 bytes the pixel data for the second line, and so on.


                                        src/yak.s            Page 7       5:01:00 pm 29-Sep-2026        RMAC 2.1.01 (Linux/OSX)


  337                                   ;
  338                                   ; So when we describe the GPU or Blitter drawing to the screen what we mean is
  339                                   ; that they are filling this region of RAM with the necessary pixel data for
  340                                   ; each screen line. 
  341                                   ;
  342                                   ; The Graphics Processor (GPU)'s speciality is performing the very fast trigonometric operations
  343                                   ; necessary to calculate the co-ordinates of a 3d object in 3d space and
  344                                   ; mapping these to the two-dimensional screen. 
  345                                   ; 
  346                                   ; The Blitter's speciality is performing bulk copy and move operations of the
  347                                   ; pixel data in the region of RAM.
  348                                   ;
  349                                   ; Neither have any concept of a physical screen or the hardware operations
  350                                   ; necessary to turn our 215040 long region of RAM into light on a physical
  351                                   ; screen. This specialty is performed by the Object Processor - it takes the
  352                                   ; region of RAM filled out by the GPU and Blitter and turns it into images on a
  353                                   ; physical screen.
  354                                   ;
  355                                   ; So at its simplest, getting our screens physically drawn is as easy as
  356                                   ; handing a pointer to the region of RAM we've populated using the GPU/Blitter
  357                                   ; and telling the Object Processor to paint it to the physical screen. In
  358                                   ; Tempest 2000 this region of RAM is whatever the address in 'gpu_screen' is
  359                                   ; pointing to. All drawing by the GPU and the Blitter is done to whatever this
  360                                   ; address is. This creates flexibility because we can change the address and
  361                                   ; thus draw to more than one region of RAM, in other words we can draw multiple
  362                                   ; different screens and decide which one to get the Object Processor to
  363                                   ; paint!
  364                                   ;
  365                                   ; For Tempest 2000, 'gpu_screen' usually contains a pointer to the region of
  366                                   ; RAM referenced by 'dscreen'.  This is a 215040 byte region of RAM at address
  367                                   ; $10034800. So for the most part this is the data we want the Object Processor
  368                                   ; to turn into images on a screen.
  369                                   ;
  370                                   ; Object Lists (aka Display Lists)
  371                                   ; ------------
  372                                   ; The actual mechanics of this are surprisingly involved. We are better to start
  373                                   ; at the end rather than the beginning.
  374                                   ;
  375                                   ; The data structure that the Jaguar hardware uses to write graphics to the screen
  376                                   ; is called an 'Object List'. This is also referred to as a 'Display List'. It is 
  377                                   ; a list of display objects, each of which contains enough information for the
  378                                   ; graphics processor to do something meaningful with. From first principles, you 
  379                                   ; can imagine that this must at least include information about position, dimensions,
  380                                   ; and the actual pixel data to be written. You would be correct. Here is an actual
  381                                   ; example of an object list used to draw a frame of the 'Game Over' screen in 
  382                                   ; Tempest 2000. There are five objects given in total below, only two of them draw
  383                                   ; to the screen: these are the 'Bit Mapped Object' entries. The first draws the
  384                                   ; 'Game Over' graphic, the second draws the player score at the top of the screen.
  385                                   ;
